		'gbuy.lead': "Luxury property in Barcelona isn't a single market. It's a handful of micro-markets, each with its own rhythm, and the difference between them matters more than the headline price. Here's what to understand before you put money down.",
		'gbuy.h2a': 'Where the money actually goes',
		'gbuy.p1': "Ask ten people where to buy \"luxury\" in Barcelona and most will say Passeig de Gràcia. Fair enough — the modernist apartment buildings there are some of the most beautiful in the world, and you pay for it. A prime apartment in the Quadrat d'Or or the upper reaches of Eixample can run well into the millions, and renovated ones with original ceilings and galleries go fast when they come up.",
		'gbuy.p2': "But there's a quieter tier above that. Sarrià-Sant Gervasi, and especially the hills around Pedralbes and Tres Torres, is where serious buyers look for space — gardens, security, discretion, and houses rather than apartments. Pedralbes in particular has long been the address that signals old money. Prices there start high and don't really have an obvious ceiling.",
		'gbuy.p3': "Then there's the coast. The Maresme towns of Alella, Teià, Cabrils and Sant Andreu de Llavaneres have quietly become the answer for buyers who want a villa with sea views and a pool, within 30 minutes of the city. You get far more house for the money than on Pedralbes' hill, and for a lot of people that's the whole appeal.",
		'gbuy.h2b': 'Prices, roughly',
		'gbuy.p4': "Numbers here move, and a \"luxury\" price tag depends enormously on street, floor, and state of repair. As a rule of thumb: a genuinely high-end apartment in prime Eixample starts around €1 million, and the better ones sail past that. A detached house with a garden in Sarrià or Pedralbes runs several million, with the hillside addresses at the top of that band. On the Maresme coast a solid villa starts lower than its Pedralbes equivalent, and the real premium is for sea views and proximity to the water.",
		'gbuy.p5': "What most buyers miss is that the listed price is only part of the story. The taxes and fees you add on top in Catalonia can be substantial, and they're easy to underestimate if you're used to lower-cost markets.",
		'gbuy.h2c': "The taxes and fees you'll actually pay",
		'gbuy.p6': "On a resale property, the main cost is the transfer tax (ITP), which in Catalonia is typically around 10% of the purchase price. New builds are different: you pay VAT (IVA) plus a smaller stamp duty (AJD). On top of either, budget for notary, land registry and legal fees — together usually a couple of percent. So on a €2 million home, assume you'll need €200,000-plus above the asking price. It's the figure that reshapes budgets.",
		'gbuy.p7': "A good lawyer is not optional here. They'll check the title, outstanding debts on the property, and make sure everything is clean before you commit. Skipping this step to save a few thousand euros is how expensive mistakes happen.",
		'gbuy.h2d': 'What to check before you commit',
		'gbuy.li1': 'Is it resale or new build? The tax treatment differs, and so does the paperwork.',
		'gbuy.li2': 'Is there a community of owners, and what are the monthly fees?',
